Ladybird accepts the three prefixed text-align values, and uses physical
left and right for their block alignment in either text direction. Blink
resolves the keyword in BlockLayoutAlgorithm, WebKit resolves inline
margins in RenderBox::computeInlineDirectionMargins(), and Gecko accepts
prefixed aliases for TextAlignKeyword.

Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 agreed on
auto margins, asymmetric fixed margins, and safe overflow in both
directions. They also mapped left and right to the top and bottom
edges in both vertical writing modes. Firefox serializes the values
with its own prefix. Ladybird serializes them with the WebKit prefix,
as Chrome and WebKit do.

Ladybird measures float exclusions at the edge where a block aligns. It
also repositions an unchanged child when the container changes its
alignment or overflow safety. Chrome 158.0.8127.36 aligned the block
within the float-free space and honored all three overflow settings, in
BlockLayoutAlgorithm and WebkitTextAlignAndJustifySelfOffset().

Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5 kept the block at its legacy alignment in
these probes. Gecko measures float exclusions in
BlockReflowState::GetFloatAvailableSpaceWithState(). WebKit passes the
space between floats to RenderBox::computeInlineDirectionMargins().
